#!/usr/bin/env ssc
---
name: rozum-public-matrix
version: 0.1.0
description: The UCC's public, view-token-gated read routes, served from ScalaScript.
---
# rozum public matrix

Slice 1 of `docs/specs/ucc-ssc-backend.md`: the two routes of the UCC console that need **no
session at all** — they carry a view token, which is a string checked against a file.

**Why these two and not the other two public ones.** `/control/public/matrix` reads
`matrix_queue()` and `/control/public/matrix/live` reads `matrix_live()`; both are process-global
state inside the gateway, and no separate server can see it. Measured before writing this, which is
why the slice is two routes rather than the four the spec first named.

**What this proves, and what it does not.** It proves a .ssc server can stand beside the Rust one
and answer real traffic from real files. It does not touch auth, spawn, PTY or launchd — those are
later slices, and mixing them in would make this one unreviewable.

**The guard came first.** `safePathSeg` is the only security-relevant line here: `stamp`, `agent`,
`task` and the normalised `model` are joined onto the results directory, so a crafted `../..` must
be REFUSED rather than sanitised. It was ported and tested on its own before a single route was
written.

```scalascript
[route, serve, Request, Response](std/http.ssc)
[readFile, exists, isDir, listDir](std/fs.ssc)
[jsonParse, jsonStringify](std/json.ssc)
[envOrElse](std/os.ssc)

// `std/os.ssc` provides the env primitive. There is no `std/env.ssc` in this runtime tier — the
// live meeting server shells out to `sh -c` for the same job, which is one reason to write this
// down rather than copy that.
def home(): String =
  envOrElse("HOME", "/tmp")

def stateRoot(): String =
  envOrElse("XDG_STATE_HOME", home() + "/.local/state") + "/rozum"

def viewTokensPath(): String = stateRoot() + "/ucc-view-tokens.json"
def uccSiteDir(): String     = home() + "/.rozum/ucc/site"

// `bench_results_dir()` in Rust is CWD-relative (`scripts/bench/results`), which is fine for a
// process started in the repo and wrong for a service. Take it from the environment so this server
// can be started from anywhere, and fall back to the same relative path so a repo-local run behaves
// as the Rust one does.
def benchResultsDir(): String =
  envOrElse("ROZUM_BENCH_RESULTS", "scripts/bench/results")

// The Rust original: non-empty, not "." or "..", no path separator. It REJECTS rather than
// sanitises, so a crafted `../../etc` cannot walk out of the directory it is joined onto.
// Written without unary `!` on a call: `build-rust` refuses that form ("unsupported expression:
// Term.ApplyUnary") while `run` accepts it, so `== false` is the spelling both paths take.
def safePathSeg(s: String): Boolean =
  s.length > 0 && s != "." && s != ".." &&
    s.contains("/") == false && s.contains("\\") == false

// The model spec carries characters that are legal in a spec and not in one path segment.
def safeModel(m: String): String =
  m.replace("/", "_").replace(":", "_").replace(" ", "_")

// A token is valid when it is present in the file and not revoked. An unreadable or absent file
// means NO token is valid — failing closed, because this is the only thing standing between the
// open internet and the console's read routes.
def viewTokenOk(t: String): Boolean =
  if t.length == 0 then false
  else
    val raw = if exists(viewTokensPath()) then readFile(viewTokensPath()) else ""
    if raw.length == 0 then false
    else
      val toks = jsonParse(raw)
      tokenListHas(toks, t)

def tokenListHas(toks: Any, t: String): Boolean =
  toks match
    case l: List[Any] => l.exists(e => tokenMatches(e, t))
    case _            => false

def tokenMatches(e: Any, t: String): Boolean =
  e match
    case m: Map[String, Any] =>
      val tok = m.get("token").map(v => v.toString).getOrElse("")
      val rev = m.get("revoked").map(v => v.toString).getOrElse("false")
      tok == t && rev != "true"
    case _ => false

// The statuses match the Rust handlers these replace, measured against them rather than chosen:
// a bad token is 403 on the matrix route and 410 on /view. Answering 200 with an error body — which
// is all this lane could do before `Response` carried a status — turns a refusal into an OK for
// anything that reads the code rather than the body.
// The content type is stated on EVERY JSON answer, including the error ones. Left unstated the
// runtime guesses from the path and guessed `text/plain` for a body that is JSON — measured against
// the Rust route, which answers `application/json` on all three. `charset` is omitted for the same
// reason: this is a drop-in replacement, so its headers match rather than improve on the original.
val JSON_CT: Map[String, String] = Map("Content-Type" -> "application/json")

def forbidden(): Response =
  Response(403, JSON_CT, jsonStringify(Map("error" -> "invalid or revoked token")))

// The 400 body carries `ok: false` and the 403 body does NOT — an inconsistency in the Rust routes,
// reproduced rather than tidied. A drop-in replacement that "fixes" a payload shape breaks whatever
// already reads it, and finding out which is not this slice's job. Built as a literal so the key
// ORDER matches too; a Map would order by its own rules.
def badRequest(msg: String): Response =
  val body = "{\"ok\":false,\"error\":" + jsonStringify(msg) + "}"
  Response(400, JSON_CT, body)

def ok(body: String): Response =
  Response(200, JSON_CT, body)

// ── The door (slice 2, docs/specs/ucc-ssc-session.md) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// This server binds a loopback TCP port — `serve(port)` is what the toolkit offers, there is no
// unix socket to hide behind — so ANY local process can reach it, including every agent this
// project launches. That does not matter for the two routes here, which are public by design and
// carry their own view token. It matters for every route after them: a gated route served this way
// would answer on :8412 what :8411 correctly answers 401 to. The door is what makes those ports
// agree, and it is built BEFORE the first gated route rather than after.
//
// It says "this came through the gate" and nothing about WHO — which the measurement in the spec
// says is sufficient, because no route handler in control.rs reads the principal at all.
//
// FAILS CLOSED, and that is the whole point of this half: with a secret configured, a request that
// does not carry it is refused, whatever it asks for. With no secret configured the door is open —
// otherwise deploying this file would take the live console down until a file exists on the host,
// which is the kind of upgrade that teaches people to skip upgrades.
// The first line of a file, for the secret below. There is no `trim` in this tier, and a secret
// written with `echo` or `printf` ends in a newline; splitting takes the value and leaves the
// newline behind. A file with leading spaces would not match — say so here rather than let someone
// discover it from a 403.
def firstLine(s: String): String =
  val parts = s.split("\n").toList
  if parts.length == 0 then "" else parts(0)

// Environment first, file second — the same order the Rust half resolves it in, and the same order
// `ROZUM_WEB_SECRET` uses, so it does not matter who started the process. Without the file branch
// the secret would have to be threaded through a plist to be usable at all, and the two halves
// would disagree about where the secret lives.
def doorSecretPath(): String = home() + "/.rozum/secrets/ucc-ssc-door"

def doorSecret(): String =
  val fromEnv = envOrElse("ROZUM_UCC_SSC_SECRET", "")
  if fromEnv != "" then fromEnv
  else if exists(doorSecretPath()) then firstLine(readFile(doorSecretPath()))
  else ""

def doorOpen(req: Request): Boolean =
  val want = doorSecret()
  want == "" || req.headers.getOrElse("x-rozum-ucc-door", "") == want

def noDoor(): Response =
  Response(403, JSON_CT, jsonStringify(Map("error" -> "this port is reachable only through the console")))

// ── /control/public/matrix/cell ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Find one cell in a matrix run: the per-run.csv row matching agent+model+task, plus the archived
// agent log (tailed) and the verifier output when they were kept.

def csvRows(path: String): List[String] =
  if exists(path) then readFile(path).split("\n").toList else []

def csvCell(header: List[String], row: List[String], name: String): String =
  val i = header.indexOf(name)
  if i < 0 || i >= row.length then "" else row(i)

def rowMatches(header: List[String], row: List[String], agent: String, model: String, task: String): Boolean =
  csvCell(header, row, "agent") == agent &&
    csvCell(header, row, "model") == model &&
    csvCell(header, row, "task") == task

// Built by hand rather than `header.zip(row).toMap`, and joined with `mkString` rather than `join`.
// `join` does not exist in the interpreter the .ssc `run` path uses — it works only when the source
// is lowered by `build-rust`, because that maps onto Rust's own `[String]::join`. `mkString` is the
// name both paths know, and it is the same behaviour. Reported upstream as
// `join-works-under-build-rust-not-run`; before that report the missing method rendered as the text
// `Stub` and SERIALISED, so this file once answered `{"cell":{Stub}}` with status 200.
// A CSV cell carries no types, and the console does: it SORTS on `seconds` and compares `pass`.
// Quoting a number turns those into string comparisons, where 9 > 524.2 — which is why this
// coerces rather than passing every field through as text. Measured against the Rust handler on
// one real row: 14 of its fields are numeric and were arriving quoted.
// A CSV field is a number when it parses as one, and the test is CANONICAL-FORM, not "parses":
// `"007"` and `"-0"` are rejected because emitting them bare would produce invalid JSON. That used
// to be expressed as a round-trip — `s.toInt.toString == s` — which said it in one line.
//
// IT CANNOT BE WRITTEN THAT WAY ANY MORE, and the reason is a fix landing upstream, not a defect.
// `toInt` on a non-integer used to diverge between the lanes: it aborted under `run` and silently
// answered 0 under `build-rust` (reported from here as `toint-on-a-non-integer-diverges`). They
// closed it by making BOTH lanes abort — the right call, and it turns the round-trip into a
// landmine: `isNumeric` is called on every CSV field, and `seconds` is `3.1`. Measured on their
// fixed main before rewriting: `isNumeric("3.1")` panics with `String.toInt: invalid integer`.
// Building is not working, and this file built fine.
//
// So the same predicate without a partial function. `toDouble` is still not the answer for the
// reason it never was: Rust prints `8.0_f64` as "8", so a round-trip through it says `"8.0"` is not
// numeric and the field stays a quoted string. Caught originally by comparing every cell of every
// bench run against the Rust handler, not by reading — one cell happened to have no such value.
//
// `val ch: String` is load-bearing, not style. `"0123456789".indexOf(s.substring(i, i + 1))` lowers
// to `.iter().position(…)` on a String, which does not compile; binding the argument to a typed
// local first lowers to the string form. Measured both ways on their main — the receiver is not
// what decides it, the ARGUMENT is.
// The walk is still character-by-character rather than anything shorter, and that is not left over
// from a defect: `toInt` is the only one-line way to say this and it ABORTS on a non-integer, which
// is exactly what `isNumeric` feeds it. The `length`-versus-`substring` unit mismatch that this walk
// was once also arranged around is fixed upstream (`string-length-counts-bytes-not-characters`),
// re-measured, so the bound is now simply correct rather than correct-by-argument.
def allDigitsFrom(s: String, i: Int): Boolean =
  if i >= s.length then true
  else
    val ch: String = s.substring(i, i + 1)
    if "0123456789".indexOf(ch) < 0 then false
    else allDigitsFrom(s, i + 1)

def isInt(s: String): Boolean =
  if s.length == 0 then false
  else
    val first: String = s.substring(0, 1)
    if first == "-" then
      if allDigitsFrom(s, 1) == false then false
      else if s.length == 1 then false
      else s.substring(1, 2) != "0"
    else
      if allDigitsFrom(s, 0) == false then false
      else if s.length == 1 then true
      else first != "0"

def isNumeric(s: String): Boolean =
  if s.length == 0 then false
  else if isInt(s) then true
  else
    val parts = s.split(".").toList
    parts.length == 2 && isInt(parts(0)) && isInt(parts(1))

def jsonScalar(s: String): String =
  if isNumeric(s) then s else jsonStringify(s)

// The task definitions, read from the file the bench serves to the model. An unreadable file or an
// unknown task answers the same SHAPE with nulls — the gateway does the same, and a console saying
// "unknown" beats one confidently showing a task the model never saw.
def benchTasksPath(): String =
  envOrElse("ROZUM_BENCH_TASKS", "scripts/bench/tasks.json")

def taskInfoUnknown(task: String): String =
  "{\"label\":" + jsonStringify(task) + ",\"difficulty\":null,\"prompt\":null,\"expected\":null}"

// The FILE's key order, not the parser's. `jsonStringify` of a parsed entry emits keys sorted
// (`difficulty, expected, label, …`) while the Rust handler clones the parsed value and its
// serde_json preserves the source order (`label, difficulty, prompt, expected, tool_hint`) — so a
// re-serialized entry is the same data and a different byte string. Every task in
// `scripts/bench/tasks.json` carries exactly these five keys in exactly this order (checked across
// all twelve), so naming them here is a description of the file rather than a guess about it; an
// unknown key would be dropped, which is why the check is written down next to the list.
def taskFieldsInFileOrder(): List[String] =
  ["label", "difficulty", "prompt", "expected", "tool_hint"]

def taskInfoJson(task: String): String =
  val path = benchTasksPath()
  if exists(path) then
    val doc = jsonParse(readFile(path))
    doc.get("tasks") match
      case Some(tasks) =>
        tasks.get(task) match
          case Some(entry) => entryInFileOrder(entry)
          case None        => taskInfoUnknown(task)
      case None => taskInfoUnknown(task)
  else taskInfoUnknown(task)

def entryInFileOrder(entry: Any): String =
  entry match
    case m: Map[String, Any] =>
      val parts = taskFieldsInFileOrder()
        .filter(k => m.get(k).map(v => true).getOrElse(false))
        // The value is stringified INSIDE the `map` over the Option: `getOrElse("")` on an
        // `Option[Any]` would default a `Value` with a `String` and does not type-check.
        .map(k => jsonStringify(k) + ":" + m.get(k).map(v => jsonStringify(v)).getOrElse("null"))
      "{" + parts.mkString(",") + "}"
    // Bound, not `entry` again: the match MOVES the scrutinee, so naming it in an arm is the only
    // way to use it there (E0382 otherwise).
    case other => jsonStringify(other)

def rowToJson(header: List[String], row: List[String]): String =
  val pairs = header.zipWithIndex.map(hi =>
    jsonStringify(hi._1) + ":" + jsonScalar(if hi._2 < row.length then row(hi._2) else "")
  )
  "{" + pairs.toList.mkString(",") + "}"

// Matches Rust's `text.lines()`, which the handler this replaces uses: a trailing newline does NOT
// produce a final empty line. `split("\n")` does, so this dropped one character more than the Rust
// answer — 1243 against 1242 — and the two were not equal even though the text read the same.
def tailLines(text: String, n: Int): String =
  val raw = text.split("\n").toList
  val ls = if raw.length > 0 && raw(raw.length - 1) == "" then raw.dropRight(1) else raw
  val start = if ls.length > n then ls.length - n else 0
  ls.drop(start).mkString("\n")

// The token branch is written positive-first — `if ok then … else forbidden()` — rather than with
// `if !viewTokenOk(t)`. `build-rust` rejects a unary `!` on a call ("unsupported expression:
// Term.ApplyUnary") while `run` accepts it, so the negated form compiles on one path only. Second
// instance of that seam found while writing this file; the first was `join`.
// Slice 3: the AUTHENTICATED twin of the route below. Same bytes, two differences, both measured
// rather than chosen: no view token (the console's `require_perm_read` layer is what admits it, and
// the door is what stops anything else), and `tail` defaults to 120 (`default_tail()`) where the
// public route defaults to 200. One body serving both is the point of the slice — the alternative
// is the same cell logic written twice, which is what this file already replaced once.
def cellAuthedRoute(req: Request): Response =
  cellBody(req, true)

def cellRoute(req: Request): Response =
  val t = req.query.get("t").getOrElse("")
  if viewTokenOk(t) then cellBody(req, false) else forbidden()

// `authed` picks BOTH differences between the two Rust handlers, and there is no third: the tail
// default (120 vs 200) and the KEY ORDER — the authenticated one puts `task_info` second, the
// public one puts it fifth. Key order is not cosmetic for a drop-in replacement; it is part of the
// bytes an existing reader already parses, and the two orders are matched rather than unified for
// the same reason the payload shapes were: fixing an inconsistency breaks whoever reads it.
def cellBody(req: Request, authed: Boolean): Response =
    val q = req.query
    val stamp = q.get("stamp").getOrElse("")
    val agent = q.get("agent").getOrElse("")
    val model = q.get("model").getOrElse("")
    val task  = q.get("task").getOrElse("")
    val sm    = safeModel(model)
    if safePathSeg(stamp) && safePathSeg(agent) && safePathSeg(task) && safePathSeg(sm) then
      // The default is the CALLER's, because the two Rust handlers disagree: the public one caps at
      // 200 and the authenticated one at `default_tail()` = 120. Two defaults for one idea, matched
      // rather than tidied — this is a drop-in replacement of both. Found by comparing whole logs
      // across runs: a 123-line log came back entire from Rust and capped from here.
      val tail = q.get("tail").map(s => s.toInt).getOrElse(if authed then 120 else 200)
      val capped = if tail > 3000 then 3000 else tail
      val rows = csvRows(benchResultsDir() + "/" + stamp + "/per-run.csv")
      if rows.length < 2 then ok(jsonStringify(Map("cell" -> "", "agent_log" -> "", "verify_out" -> "")))
      else
        val header = rows(0).split(",").toList
        val hit = rows.drop(1).filter(r => r.length > 0)
          .find(r => rowMatches(header, r.split(",").toList, agent, model, task))
        val cellDir = benchResultsDir() + "/" + stamp + "/cells/" + agent + "/" + sm + "/" + task
        val logPath = cellDir + "/agent.log"
        val verPath = cellDir + "/verify.out"
        // ABSENT is null, not "". The console distinguishes "this cell kept no logs" from "the log
        // is empty", and the Rust handler it replaces returns null — measured on a real cell whose
        // run did not keep its workdir.
        val agentLog = if exists(logPath) then jsonStringify(tailLines(readFile(logPath), capped)) else "null"
        val verifyOut = if exists(verPath) then jsonStringify(readFile(verPath)) else "null"
        val cellJson = hit.map(r => rowToJson(header, r.split(",").toList)).getOrElse("null")
        // `triage_out`, `has_logs` and `task_info` are the Rust handler's, matched here because
        // the console reads them. `task_info` used to be missing on purpose: the gateway kept the
        // task definitions in a compiled table and a second copy here would have drifted from it.
        // It no longer does — `scripts/bench/tasks.json` is the one source both read, so this
        // reads the same file the bench serves to the model.
        val triagePath = cellDir + "/triage.out"
        val triageOut = if exists(triagePath) then jsonStringify(readFile(triagePath)) else "null"
        val hasLogs = if exists(cellDir) then "true" else "false"
        // ONE order, because the two Rust handlers agree with each other — and this file did not
        // agree with EITHER. `task_info` belongs second; it sat fifth here, which means the public
        // route has been serving a different byte string from the handler it replaced ever since
        // `task_info` was added to it. Yesterday's 1975-cell acceptance could not see that: it
        // compared this server against ITSELF across a deploy. Comparing against Rust, live and
        // side by side, is what found it.
        ok("{\"cell\":" + cellJson +
          ",\"task_info\":" + taskInfoJson(task) +
          ",\"agent_log\":" + agentLog +
          ",\"verify_out\":" + verifyOut +
          ",\"triage_out\":" + triageOut +
          ",\"has_logs\":" + hasLogs + "}")
    else badRequest("invalid stamp/agent/model/task segment")

// ── /chat/messages and /chat/incidents ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Slice 3. Both go straight to the room files on disk — the gateway's own doc comment says so, and
// it is why they are portable at all: nothing here can see the console's process state.
//
// The room's directory is NOT derived from its name. `rooms.json` maps name → root, so a room can
// live anywhere, and reading that mapping is what makes this a port rather than a guess about
// layout. `state_dir()` on the Rust side is `rozum_paths::state_dir()`, which is the same
// `XDG_STATE_HOME`-or-`~/.local/state` rule `stateRoot()` already implements above.

def roomsJsonPath(): String = stateRoot() + "/rooms.json"

// Non-empty, at most 128, and none of CR / LF / NUL / `/`. Matched to the Rust guard exactly: it
// is what keeps a room name from walking out of the mapping, and a stricter version here would
// answer 400 where the console answers 200.
def validRoomName(n: String): Boolean =
  n.length > 0 && n.length <= 128 &&
    n.contains("/") == false && n.contains("\n") == false && n.contains("\r") == false

def roomRootIn(entries: Any, name: String): String =
  entries match
    case l: List[Any] =>
      // Indexed rather than `headOption`: this lane lowers a parenless collection member as a Rust
      // FIELD access and refuses it, which the compiler says in so many words.
      // `filter` + `mkString`, and the reason is NOT what an earlier version of this comment said.
      // It blamed `take(n)` after `map` for returning empty; that was wrong — the mapped list
      // begins with an empty string (the first room is not the one being looked up), so `take(1)`
      // returned exactly what it should have, and the empty answer came from the type-pattern
      // defect above. `filter` + `mkString` is exact here because `rooms.json` is a name→root
      // MAPPING: at most one entry matches, so joining the matches cannot differ from taking the
      // first.
      l.map(e => roomRootOf(e, name)).filter(r => r.length > 0).mkString("")
    case _ => ""

def roomRootOf(e: Any, name: String): String =
  e match
    case m: Map[String, Any] =>
      val nm = m.get("name").map(v => v.toString).getOrElse("")
      if nm == name then m.get("root").map(v => v.toString).getOrElse("") else ""
    case _ => ""

def roomRoot(name: String): String =
  val path = roomsJsonPath()
  if exists(path) == false then ""
  else
    val parsed = jsonParse(readFile(path))
    parsed match
      // Two shapes, both accepted by the Rust reader: a bare array, or `{ "rooms": [...] }`.
      //
      // ARRAY FIRST, and the order is load-bearing rather than stylistic: in the rust lane a JSON
      // ARRAY also matches `case m: Map[String, Any]`, so with the object arm first every bare-array
      // `rooms.json` — which is the shape this machine actually has — took the object branch, found
      // no `rooms` key, and returned "no such room" for every room. Measured with a probe running
      // this exact function both ways; the interpreter picks the List arm and is right. `tokenListHas`
      // above has always matched the list first, which is why the deployed routes never hit this.
      case l: List[Any]        => roomRootIn(l, name)
      case m: Map[String, Any] => m.get("rooms").map(v => roomRootIn(v, name)).getOrElse("")
      case _                   => ""

// `ts` is unix seconds; the Rust reader formats HH:MM in UTC by plain arithmetic, with no timezone
// and no date. Ported as arithmetic for the same reason — a `Date` here would render the operator's
// local time and silently disagree with the console it replaces.
def two(n: Int): String =
  if n < 10 then "0" + n.toString else n.toString

def hhmm(ts: Int): String =
  two((ts / 3600) % 24) + ":" + two((ts / 60) % 60)

// One message line → its JSON object, or "" when the line is not one. A line without `content` is
// SKIPPED rather than defaulted: the Rust reader drops it, and a placeholder would put a message
// in the console that nobody wrote.
def messageJson(line: String): String =
  val parsed = jsonParse(line)
  parsed match
    // `map` over the Option, so an ABSENT `content` and an EMPTY one stay distinguishable: the Rust
    // reader skips the first and keeps the second, and a `strField` returning "" for both would
    // have quietly dropped every empty message.
    // Only STRINGS cross this boundary. Passing the `Map[String, Any]` itself made the rust lane
    // emit `(m.clone()).into_iter()` on a `Value`, which does not compile — a divergence, not a
    // dialect rule, and cheap to route around by resolving the fields at the call site.
    case m: Map[String, Any] =>
      // Every read is `m.get(...)` INSIDE this arm. Two rules of this lane meet here and leave one
      // shape standing: a `Map[String, Any]` passed to a def is lowered as an iteration over a
      // `Value` (does not compile), and a value passed to a def is MOVED, so a second read of the
      // same binding is a borrow-after-move. Only helpers taking Strings and Ints are called.
      val content = m.get("content").map(v => v.toString)
      val display = m.get("display_name").map(v => v.toString).getOrElse("")
      val author2 = m.get("author").map(v => v.toString).getOrElse("")
      val ts = m.get("ts").map(v => v.toString.toInt).getOrElse(0)
      val author = if display.length > 0 then display
                   else if author2.length > 0 then author2
                   else "?"
      // ABSENT is skipped, EMPTY is kept — the Rust reader drops a line with no `content` and
      // keeps one whose content is "". Only the Option can tell those apart.
      content.map(c => messageOf(author, ts, c)).getOrElse("")
    case _ => ""

def messageOf(author: String, ts: Int, content: String): String =
  "{\"time\":" + jsonStringify(hhmm(ts)) +
    ",\"author\":" + jsonStringify(author) +
    ",\"content\":" + jsonStringify(content) + "}"

// The files are read and JOINED before parsing, rather than mapped file-by-file: `flatMap` has no
// lowering in this lane, and joining preserves the same order (name-sorted files, lines in order)
// that the Rust reader produces by appending as it goes.
def jsonlLines(root: String, files: List[String]): List[String] =
  files.map(f => if exists(root + "/" + f) then readFile(root + "/" + f) else "")
    .mkString("\n").split("\n").toList

def isJsonl(name: String): Boolean =
  name.length > 6 && name.substring(name.length - 6, name.length) == ".jsonl"

def chatMessagesRoute(req: Request): Response =
  val room = req.query.get("room").getOrElse("")
  if validRoomName(room) == false then Response(400, JSON_CT, "")
  else
    val root = roomRoot(room)
    if root.length == 0 || isDir(root) == false then ok("[]")
    else
      // Sorted by NAME, which is what the Rust reader does and what makes the daily files read in
      // date order (`2026-08-16.jsonl`). Then the LAST 80 across all files, not per file.
      val files = listDir(root).filter(f => isJsonl(f)).sorted
      // ONLY lines that look like a JSON object reach `jsonParse`, and that is not tidiness: in
      // the rust lane `jsonParse` PANICS on input it cannot parse ("EOF while parsing a value"),
      // the http runtime's mutex is then poisoned, and every later request on that server dies
      // with `PoisonError` — one blank line at the end of one `.jsonl` file killed the whole
      // process for good. The Rust reader trims and skips empties; this skips anything that does
      // not start with `{`, which is the same set for a message log and cannot panic.
      val objects = jsonlLines(root, files).filter(l => l.length > 0 && l.substring(0, 1) == "{")
      val all = objects.map(l => messageJson(l)).filter(j => j.length > 0)
      // The count is taken BEFORE the drop: `all.drop(all.length - 80)` moves the list and then
      // reads its length, which the generated Rust rejects (E0382).
      val n = all.length
      val keep = if n > 80 then all.drop(n - 80) else all
      ok("[" + keep.mkString(",") + "]")

// `threads.json`, one object per incident. Field order is the Rust struct's declaration order —
// severity, state, title, owner — because that is what serde emits and this is a drop-in.
def incidentJson(e: Any): String =
  e match
    // Same Option-`map` as the messages: an incident with no `title` is dropped by the Rust reader,
    // and only the Option can say "absent" apart from "empty".
    case m: Map[String, Any] =>
      val title = m.get("title").map(v => v.toString)
      val sev   = m.get("severity").map(v => v.toString).getOrElse("low")
      val state = m.get("state").map(v => v.toString).getOrElse("open")
      val owner = m.get("owner").map(v => v.toString).getOrElse("")
      title.map(t => incidentOf(sev, state, t, owner)).getOrElse("")
    case _ => ""

def incidentOf(sev: String, state: String, title: String, owner: String): String =
  "{\"severity\":" + jsonStringify(sev) +
    ",\"state\":" + jsonStringify(state) +
    ",\"title\":" + jsonStringify(title) +
    ",\"owner\":" + jsonStringify(owner) + "}"

def incidentsIn(parsed: Any): List[String] =
  parsed match
    case l: List[Any] => l.map(e => incidentJson(e)).filter(j => j.length > 0)
    case _            => []

def chatIncidentsRoute(req: Request): Response =
  val room = req.query.get("room").getOrElse("")
  if validRoomName(room) == false then Response(400, JSON_CT, "")
  else
    val root = roomRoot(room)
    val path = root + "/threads.json"
    if root.length == 0 || exists(path) == false then ok("[]")
    else
      // Same guard as the messages: an empty or truncated `threads.json` must answer `[]` like the
      // Rust reader, not panic and poison the server.
      val raw = readFile(path)
      if raw.length == 0 || (raw.substring(0, 1) != "[" && raw.substring(0, 1) != "{") then ok("[]")
      else ok("[" + incidentsIn(jsonParse(raw)).mkString(",") + "]")

// ── /view/{token} ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The read-only console page. The token is the LAST path segment, and it is checked before the file
// is read — an invalid token must not even reveal whether the page exists.

def lastSegment(path: String): String =
  val parts = path.split("/").toList
  if parts.length == 0 then "" else parts(parts.length - 1)

// A prefix route still receives the whole Request — the route is chosen before the handler runs and
// `path` carries every segment, so the token is readable from it.
//
// The page served here is `view.html`, NOT the console's `index.html`: they are different pages, and
// serving the wrong one answers 200 with 580KB of the wrong thing. Status parity does not notice
// that, which is how it survived a round of checking.
val HEAD_END: String = "</head>"

def expiredPage(): String =
  "<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>rozum \u00b7 link expired</title></head>" +
  "<body style='font:16px system-ui;text-align:center;padding:60px;background:#0f1117;color:#c9d1d9'>" +
  "This link is invalid or has been revoked.</body></html>"

def viewRoute(req: Request): Response =
  val t = lastSegment(req.path)
  // `/view/` with nothing after it is NOT an expired link, and the Rust route answers 404 with an
  // empty body. Found by comparing the two servers on the empty token — the earlier round compared
  // a live one and a revoked one and never asked what a bare prefix does. Both refuse either way,
  // so this was never a hole; it was an unmeasured difference, which is its own kind of debt.
  if t == "" then Response(404, Map(), "")
  else if viewTokenOk(t) then
    val page = uccSiteDir() + "/view.html"
    if exists(page) then
      // The page has no other way to learn its token: it is injected as a script variable before
      // </head>. Serving the file unpatched is a 200 carrying a console that cannot call anything.
      val html = readFile(page)
      val inject = "<script>window._VIEW_TOKEN='" + t + "';</script>"
      // Insert before the FIRST `</head>`, which is what the Rust route does — every later one is
      // content and survives verbatim.
      //
      // This was `split` plus a hand-rolled join, and every concatenation joined exactly TWO
      // operands, because `indexOf` on a String lowered to `.iter().position(…)` and a three-operand
      // chain lowered to `String + String`. Both were reported from here and both are fixed
      // upstream, so the ordinary shape is back. Re-measured on their main before this change
      // rather than taken from the changelog: `indexOf` answers 12 on both lanes for the same
      // input, and a three-operand concatenation compiles and prints `abc`.
      //
      // The tail is bound to a local rather than written inline as a third operand, and that is the
      // one piece of the old shape that has to stay. `a + b + c` compiles now when the operands are
      // plain, but with a CALL on the right it still lowers to `format!(..) + String`, which wants a
      // `&str`. Measured on their main, all four variants in one file: plain three-operand ok,
      // rightmost-call E0308, rightmost bound to a typed local ok, two-step ok.
      // The local lives INSIDE the else, not above the branch: `at` is -1 when the page carries no
      // `</head>`, and `substring(-1, …)` is evaluated eagerly if it sits outside. That is a fault
      // the split-based shape could not have — worth the two extra lines.
      // Insert before the FIRST `</head>`; every later one is content and survives verbatim.
      //
      // This is the ordinary shape, and it took four upstream fixes to be able to write it. It was
      // `split` plus a hand-rolled recursive join, with every concatenation limited to two operands,
      // because `indexOf` on a String lowered to `.iter().position(…)`, a three-operand chain
      // lowered to `String + String`, and — the one that actually panicked mid-request —
      // `String.length` answered BYTES while `substring` counted CHARACTERS. All four reported from
      // here and all four fixed. Re-measured on their main before this rewrite rather than taken
      // from a status field: `length` now answers 4 for `"a\u00e9\u00b7b"` on BOTH lanes (it answered 6
      // here), and `substring(1, length)` returns the tail instead of panicking.
      //
      // The tail is still bound INSIDE the else: `at` is -1 when the page carries no `</head>`, and
      // a local above the branch would be evaluated eagerly.
      val at: Int = html.indexOf(HEAD_END)
      val patched =
        if at < 0 then inject + html
        else
          val tail: String = html.substring(at, html.length)
          html.substring(0, at) + inject + tail
      Response(200, Map("Content-Type" -> "text/html; charset=utf-8", "Cache-Control" -> "no-store"), patched)
    else Response(404, Map("Content-Type" -> "text/plain; charset=utf-8"), "view.html not found")
  else
    // 410, not 403: a link that USED to work reads better as Gone. The body is the same styled page
    // the Rust route serves — an expired link must not answer with raw JSON.
    Response(410, Map("Content-Type" -> "text/html; charset=utf-8"), expiredPage())

def main(): Unit =
  // The door is checked BEFORE the route's own rules, on every route, so adding a route cannot
  // forget it. `guard` wrapping each handler rather than a middleware because the toolkit has no
  // middleware hook — measured, not preferred.
  route("GET", "/control/public/matrix/cell", req => if doorOpen(req) then cellRoute(req) else noDoor())
  route("GET", "/view/", r => if doorOpen(r) then viewRoute(r) else noDoor())
  // Slice 3 — the gated read routes. They carry no token of their own: the console's
  // `require_perm_read` layer admits the caller and the door is what stops anything else reaching
  // this port. That order is the whole of `docs/specs/ucc-ssc-session.md`.
  route("GET", "/control/matrix/cell", req => if doorOpen(req) then cellAuthedRoute(req) else noDoor())
  route("GET", "/chat/messages", req => if doorOpen(req) then chatMessagesRoute(req) else noDoor())
  route("GET", "/chat/incidents", req => if doorOpen(req) then chatIncidentsRoute(req) else noDoor())
  serve(8412)
main()
```
